No. `CHECK (price > 0)` does not reject NULL prices. A check constraint passes when its expression evaluates to true or to NULL. With a NULL `price`, `price > 0` evaluates to NULL, so the row is accepted. To keep NULLs out, add a `NOT NULL` constraint on the column, for example `price numeric NOT NULL CHECK (price > 0)`.

The docs say this directly: most expressions return NULL when any operand is NULL, so a check constraint won't stop NULLs in the column. They name the not-null constraint as the way to prevent them. The `NOT NULL` example in the docs is on `product_no` and `name`, not `price`. I wrote the combined form myself.

